    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d134">

                <pb facs="0191" n="1025" sameAs="frus:frus1918Supp01v02/pg1025"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 763.72112A/725</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Ambassador in Great
                        Britain</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Page</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1918-04-05T21:00:00-04:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">April 5, 1918, 9 p.m.</hi>
                        </date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>7155. For Sheldon [from War Trade Board]:</p>
                <p>No. 264. Frothingham. Embassy’s 9131, March 20, 1 p.m., your 310. Impossible to
                    include names enemy trading list on basis nationality as sole ultimate fact.
                    When enemy nationality exists, however, particularly in cases in Scandinavia, it
                    may be possible to draw inferences which will diminish greatly amount of other
                    evidence necessary to add name. In view of European trade conditions and of
                    instructions in my 235, March 22 [<hi rendition="#italic">23</hi>], cases will
                    undoubtedly be rare where there is not circumstantial evidence of business done
                    within enemy lines or trade for benefit of enemy sufficient to include enemy
                    trading list under our rules such names as are passed for B[ritish] S[tatutory]
                    L[ist] under British rules.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Lansing</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
